{
  "id": "COMPOUND:228 - DISEASE:1010",
  "verbalization": "Nevirapine is implicated in the development of Enterocolitis, Pseudomembranous, through a mechanism involving the inhibition of dehydrogenase/reductase 7C, which is linked to the pathogenesis of this condition. This pathway is supported by multiple evidence chains, all of which converge on the same core mechanism: nevirapine affects a gene product that, when inhibited, leads to the development of pseudomembranous colitis. The evidence suggests that nevirapine disrupts the function of dehydrogenase/reductase 7C, which is associated with the immune system and may contribute to inflammation and tissue damage in the colon. The presence of multiple independent pathways pointing to the same gene product and resulting condition supports a robust mechanistic link between nevirapine and pseudomembranous colitis. The consistency of the evidence across different paths strengthens the conclusion that nevirapine may be a potential reagent for this condition, though further clinical validation is needed to confirm its therapeutic utility.",
